<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrei II of Vladimir</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1222–<a href="/years/1264">1264</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Andrei Yaroslavich</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrei II of Vladimir (Andrei Yaroslavich) was grand prince of Vladimir from 1249 to 1252, a son of Yaroslav II and younger brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>. Appointed by the Mongol court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, he tried to act independently of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>; in 1252 a Mongol army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevryuy">Nevryuy</a> defeated him and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. He later returned and held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> until his death in 1264.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrey Yaroslavich was the third son of Yaroslav II. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novgorod-first-chronicle">Novgorod First Chronicle</a>, the Novgorodians expelled his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> from the city in the winter of 1240 to 1241 and asked Yaroslav to send another commander, and Andrey was sent in Alexander's place. In 1241 he joined his Suzdalian forces with Alexander's, and they retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>, raided the Estonian countryside and fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-on-the-ice">Battle on the Ice</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The chronicles give different versions of how Andrey became prince of Vladimir. In one, after their father's death in 1247 Andrey and Alexander travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, where Andrey was appointed grand prince by a charter of the great khan Güyük; on their return in 1249 they found that their younger brother Mikhail Khorobrit had seized Vladimir, but he was killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> within months. In another, Andrey took the throne from his uncle Sviatoslav in 1248. In the winter of 1250 to 1251 he married Ustynia, a daughter of Danylo of Halych, who by 1251 appears to have been forming an anti-Mongol coalition.</p><p>When the great khan Güyük died in 1251, Andrey was expected to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and have his charter renewed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> in the name of the new great khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>. He refused, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> complied. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took this as a challenge: Batu's son Sartak ordered a punitive expedition, and Andrey was defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a> and fled by way of Novgorod to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Alexander then received the throne of Vladimir from the Mongols in 1252. Modern scholars read these events as a rivalry between the brothers, with Andrey seeking an anti-Mongol alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1255 Andrey returned from exile and went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> to ask pardon. He received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> and, from Alexander, the easternmost lands of Vladimir, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. After Alexander died in 1263 Andrey hoped to add Vladimir to his possessions, but his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-of-tver">Yaroslav of Tver</a> thwarted him. He died in 1264. His sons included Yury, Mikhail and Vasily of Suzdal, and the house of Nizhny Novgorod-Suzdal, known since the 14th century as the House of Shuysky, descends from him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrey II of Vladimir” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
